RD05 v36 CPU-port, RGMII timing and persistent overlay correction

Network datapath
----------------
The stable topology remains RTL8197F physical P0/RGMII to RTL8367D CPU7/EXT1.
Linux DSA supplies and consumes the 8-byte rtl8_4 tag. Hardware TX insertion
and RX tag removal therefore remain disabled on the RTL8197F side.

v33 hardware confirmation:
- CPUICR1 accepted 0x002081d3 after DMA start (8-dword TX/RX descriptors);
- CPURPDCR0 and CPUTPDCR0 accepted the actual ring bases;
- the diagnostic window contained no new peer/LAN2 traffic, so it could not
  prove or disprove switch-to-SoC RX packet delivery.

The same trace recorded FCS errors on RTL8367D CPU7 while receiving frames from
the SoC. v36 returns the non-SSC SDK switch-mode fallback TX0/RX2. This changes neither DSA tags, VLAN
membership, descriptor format nor the switch-to-SoC receive direction.

Expected markers after LAN is brought up:
  RD05 DSA master v36: ...
  rd05 p0 rgmii v36: ... cputag-rx=1 cputag-tx=1 preserve-tag=1 ...
  rd05 poststart v36: ... cpuicr1=0x002081d3 rxbase=... txbase=...

Persistent overlay
------------------
Only the RD05 rootfs_data range is writable:
  offset 0x00e60000
  size   0x001a0000

The v33 log returned the identical raw status word 0x00000004 after every WREN.
That does not prove which status bit or command write width the controller uses.
v36 therefore calibrates non-destructively before the first erase/program:

  WRDI -> RDSR -> WREN -> RDSR -> WRDI -> RDSR -> WREN -> RDSR

It tests the Realtek GPL SDK command placement (32-bit high byte) first, then
8-, 16- and 32-bit-low alternatives. A clear/set/clear/set transition must be
observed in the same status bit; static protection bits cannot pass this test.
No erase or page program is issued until calibration succeeds.

Expected success marker:
  calibrated SPI-NOR WEL: cmd=... lane=... bit=... before=... after=...

Expected overlay result:
  /dev/mtd8 exists
  /overlay is mounted from rootfs_data/JFFS2
  no "unable to calibrate SPI-NOR WEL" or JFFS2 errno -5 messages

Run:
  rd05-v36-check
  rd05-netdiag full

Use `tools/rd05-pc-netdiag.py` from the source tree on the PC during the full traffic window. A router cannot generate
proof of inbound LAN traffic by itself.


Native PCIe and WLAN boundary
-----------------------------
The GPL RTL8197F BSP reset/MDIO/config-window sequence is now implemented by
pcie-rtl8197f.c through Linux's generic PCI host-bridge API. RD05 enables the
40 MHz and RTL8197FH/VG PHY tuning. OEM Linux identifies the directly attached
endpoint as PCI 10ec:f812 and Hardware type RTL8812FE.

Expected host marker:
  rtl8197f-pcie ... native host ready: endpoint=10ec:f812 ...

The supplied WLAN implementation is the legacy vendor rtl8192cd fullmac driver
plus a Linux-5.2 backports stack. It is inventoried in RD05_SDK_SOURCE_MAP.tsv,
but is deliberately not presented as a native Linux-6.6/mac80211 driver. The
PCIe host and calibration/MAC data are ready for a future native RTL8812FE
wireless driver without importing Realtek fastpath or private netfilter hooks.


V36 hardware-derived correction
-------------------------------
The paired July 25 v35 router/PC trace proved both directions were blocked:
CPU7 counted SoC-origin frames as FCS/drop, while PC broadcasts left CPU7 but
never advanced CPURPDCR0. The exact RTL8367D DAL clears bit 2 of register
0x03f7 whenever EXT1 is not TMII. V36 performs that missing write, does not
program the RTL8367B-only 0x13f9 timing field, and exposes all four RGMII delay
axes plus SSC through rd05-rgmii-calibrate.

Default profile: SoC TX0/RX6, switch TX0/RX2, SSC off.
Measured profiles can be saved in /etc/rd05-rgmii.conf.
